"lora_enc_meshtastic_title" = "Meshtastic encryption";
"lora_enc_meshtastic_body" = "• Channel: all radios of a channel share one key (AES-128/256 in CTR mode, no integrity check). The default “LongFast” channel uses the publicly known key “AQ==” — anyone with the Meshtastic app can read such a message.\n• Direct message (firmware 2.5+): the radios encrypt it with the nodes' keys (X25519 + AES-256-CCM). The destination radio and every phone connected to it can read it.\n• The packet header (sender, recipient, ID, hop count) is not encrypted — the mesh sees who writes to whom and when.";
"lora_enc_psst_title" = "psst encryption (on top)";
"lora_enc_psst_body" = "• Every {app} user has their own X25519 key pair in the iPhone's Keychain (it never leaves it and is not backed up).\n• The text of a direct message to a linked contact is encrypted with ChaCha20-Poly1305 using a key derived from both keys (HKDF-SHA256, separate for each direction). Only the other phone can read it — neither the radios nor anyone else in the mesh.\n• Sender verification: only the holder of the contact's private key can create the message. A message counter protects against replay (a repeated message is rejected).\n• A longer message is split into several LoRa packets (max. 8) and the other phone puts it back together.\n• You exchange the keys and the “account ↔ LoRa node” binding in an encrypted {app} chat or in person with a QR code. Compare the key fingerprints.\n• Limits: no forward secrecy (a compromised key also reveals old captured messages), the Meshtastic header metadata stays visible, messages in channels get no psst encryption.";
